---
import './keys.css';
---
<figure class="key-figure">
 <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 3</span>A hit still needs permission</div>
 <div class="scene">
  <p class="label">Separate example: an access-restricted product view</p>
  <div class="path"><div class="box"><strong>Reader asks for P7</strong><span>Authenticated caller and requested shop</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><strong>Check access now</strong><span>May this caller read this view?</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><strong>If denied: stop</strong><span>Return no restricted card.</span></div></div>
  <p>Only the allowed path continues ↓</p>
  <div class="pair"><div class="box"><strong>Cache hit</strong><span>Validate the shared data, then build the permitted view.</span></div><div class="box"><strong>Cache miss</strong><span>Read the source, save the shared data, then build the permitted view.</span></div></div>
  <p class="result">The same access check protects both paths. Finding bytes under a shop’s prefix proves nothing about the caller.</p>
 </div>
 <figcaption>This example shares underlying data among authorised readers. A cached response that already contains a reader’s filtered fields needs the corresponding audience in its identity as well. The main shop catalogue remains public.</figcaption>
</figure>
